   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-12",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-780583",
   "speaker": "Sussan Ley",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-12",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-781143",
   "speaker": "Eric Abetz",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-19",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The minister moved that the bills proceed without formalities, be taken together and read a first time, and indicated they would later be listed separately on the Notice Paper."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-781144",
   "speaker": "Eric Abetz",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-19",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The Coalition Government introduced five appropriation bills for 2007-2008, seeking a total of approximately $70 billion from the Consolidated Revenue Fund, including $170.7 million for parliamentary departments, $59 billion for ordinary annual services, and $10.1 billion for other agency expenses, along with supplementary appropriations of $814 million for 2006-2007. Redundant departmental items adjustment provisions were removed from the bills, and an additional $250 million was allocated to the AusLink Strategic Regional Programme under the Department of Transport and Regional Services."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-781483",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-21",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Senator Murray supported the five appropriation bills totalling approximately $70 billion, but criticised the lack of transparency and accountability in government spending, noting that annual appropriations now account for less than 20% of total funds. He highlighted a $1.7 billion spend on government advertising since 1996, described as a \"multi-million dollar swindle,\" and argued that taxpayer-funded residences like Kirribilli House should not be used for party political fundraising."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-781484",
   "speaker": "Penny Wong",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-21",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Senator Wong argued that the government had spent $1.8 billion on political advertising and used taxpayer-funded official residences for fundraising, and announced amendments requiring Auditor-General assessment of advertising projects over $250,000 and prohibiting political fundraising at Kirribilli House and The Lodge."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-781485",
   "speaker": null,
   "party": null,
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-21",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": null
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-781486",
   "speaker": "Penny Wong",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2007-06-21",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Two amendments were moved: one requiring Auditor-General certification for any government advertising project costing $250,000 or more, citing $55 million spent on Work Choices, $52 million on private health funds, and $69 million on superannuation policies; the other prohibiting the use of taxpayer-funded official residences (Kirribilli House and The Lodge) for political fundraising. The government opposed both amendments."
  }
 ],
